.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-SET-EGRESS-LIMIT 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-set-egress-limit \- set how fast one application may call an outside site
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
.B eap set-egress-limit
\fB\-\-application\-id\fR \fIid\fR
\fB\-\-site\fR \fIhost\fR|\fB*\fR
[\fB\-\-requests\-per\-second\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-burst\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-concurrent\fR \fIn\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Sets an application's egress limit for one outside site: how many
requests per second, and how many at once, the application may make to
it - all of its instances on all nodes counted together.
.PP
A site that protects itself against denial of service counts per source
address, and every instance of an application reaches it from the same
one. A limit kept inside the application counts per instance, which the
autoscaler multiplies exactly when there's the most to fetch. So the
limit is kept in EAP, and the application asks for a permit before each
request it makes to the site - with the euclid-jdk
.BR EuclidEgressLimiter :
.PP
.RS
.nf
try (var permit = limiter.acquire(uri)) {
    response = http.send(request, ...);
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Limits are per application. Two applications calling the same site each
have their own bucket, slots and limits: one that has to go gently with
a site doesn't slow down another calling it too.
.PP
A permit is a token from the bucket (the rate) and a slot among the
application's requests to the site in flight (the concurrency). The slot
is held until the permit is closed, or until its lease runs out if the
instance holding it dies. When the site answers 429 or 503, the
application reports it, and none of its instances gets a permit for that
site until the site's
.I Retry-After
has passed (at most an hour per report).
.PP
Only applications that ask are held to the limit; euclid doesn't
intercept outgoing traffic.
.PP
What a site is held to, from the most specific:
.IP 1. 3
The application's limit for that site, set with
.BR "\-\-site " \fIhost\fR.
.IP 2. 3
The application's limit for all its sites, set with
.BR "\-\-site '*'" .
.IP 3. 3
.IR euclid.modules.eap.egress.default :
.RS
.nf
"egress": { "default": { "requests-per-second": 2, "burst": 2, "concurrent": 4 } }
.fi
.RE
.PP
A limit applies to every EAP instance at once and survives a restart.
The caller must be an administrator. The application is looked up in
the caller's account and namespace.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fIid\fR
The application the limit is for.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-site " " \fIhost\fR|\fB*\fR
The site's host name, e.g. \fIdownloads.example.com\fR. Case and a
trailing dot don't matter; the port and path aren't part of it.
\fB*\fR (quoted, so the shell leaves it alone) sets the application's
default for every site without a limit of its own.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-requests\-per\-second " " \fIn\fR
Requests per second, averaged, for all the application's instances
together. May be a fraction: \fI0.5\fR is one every two seconds.
\fI0\fR for no rate limit.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-burst " " \fIn\fR
How many requests may go in quick succession before the rate applies;
at least 1.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-concurrent " " \fIn\fR
Requests to the site in flight at once. \fI0\fR for no limit.
.PP
At least one of the last three is required; those not given keep what
is in force.
.SH OUTPUT
The application, the site and the limits now in force, as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
The downloader, one download at a time from one site, at most one start
every two seconds:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-egress-limit --application-id downloader \e
    --site downloads.example.com --requests-per-second 0.5 --burst 1 --concurrent 1
.fi
.RE
.PP
And at most two at a time from any other site:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eap set-egress-limit --application-id downloader --site '*' --concurrent 2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-list-egress-sites (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-delete-egress-limit (1)
